    taskDesc: "Design and evaluate descriptive assessments.",
    concept: {
      title: "Descriptive Assessment: ABC Data and Scatter Plot Methodology",
      body: "Descriptive assessments observe behavior as it naturally occurs, recording antecedents, behaviors, and consequences without experimentally manipulating any variable. They occupy a middle position in the FBA continuum — more informative than indirect assessment but less definitive than functional analysis.\n\nABC recording documents the sequential three-term contingency surrounding each behavioral instance. Narrative ABC recording produces rich contextual data but is difficult to quantify. Structured ABC recording — using checkboxes for predefined antecedent and consequence categories — produces data that can be summarized across observations, revealing patterns in what precedes and follows the target behavior. The critical interpretive limitation: ABC data identifies correlations, not contingencies. A consequence appearing frequently after behavior does not confirm it is reinforcing that behavior — only functional analysis can establish that relationship.\n\nScatter plot assessment, developed by Touchette, MacDonald, and Langer (1985), records whether behavior occurred during each time block across sessions and days. When analyzed across multiple sessions, scatter plots reveal temporal patterns — time-of-day effects, activity-specific clusters, day-of-week patterns — that suggest setting events and antecedent conditions not captured by ABC recording alone. Scatter plots are particularly useful for identifying when to schedule antecedent interventions and when to probe for setting event effects.\n\nDescriptive assessment findings should always be framed as hypotheses in written FBA reports. The phrasing 'behavior appears to be maintained by...' is more defensible than 'behavior is maintained by...' when the evidence base is descriptive rather than experimental."
    },
    research: {
      citation: "Lerman, D. C., & Iwata, B. A. (1993). Descriptive and experimental analyses of variables maintaining self-injurious behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 26(3), 293–319.",
      summary: "Lerman and Iwata directly compared the outcomes of descriptive assessments with functional analyses conducted with the same participants, providing one of the field's most methodologically rigorous evaluations of descriptive assessment validity. Their findings were sobering: descriptive data frequently failed to identify the correct maintaining variable and in several cases suggested a function that was directly contradicted by the experimental analysis. The most common error was identifying social positive reinforcement (attention) as the function when the experimental analysis revealed social negative reinforcement (escape) or automatic reinforcement. Lerman and Iwata concluded that descriptive assessments cannot be relied upon to identify behavioral function independently of experimental verification — a finding that remains the primary empirical justification for functional analysis as the gold standard in FBA methodology."
    },
    application: "When writing an FBA, explicitly frame all descriptive findings as hypotheses. Note which ABC patterns emerged and what they suggest, then specify how those hypotheses will be tested — either through additional structured observation or through a functional analysis. The transition from hypothesis to established function requires experimental verification, not just accumulation of descriptive data.",
    questions: [
      { q: "A scatter plot assessment is most useful for:", options: ["A. Confirming which consequence is reinforcing the problem behavior", "B. Establishing a functional relationship between the IV and the DV", "C. Calculating interobserver agreement between two observers", "D. Identifying temporal patterns in behavior across time blocks"], answer: "D", rationale: "Scatter plots reveal when behavior is most and least likely to occur across time blocks and days — identifying temporal patterns that suggest setting event and antecedent influences." },
      { q: "Lerman and Iwata (1993) found that, compared to functional analysis, descriptive assessments frequently:", options: ["A. Isolated the maintaining variable as precisely as a functional analysis", "B. Misidentified or entirely failed to detect the maintaining variable", "C. Yielded enough evidence to design treatment without functional analysis", "D. Overidentified automatic reinforcement as the maintaining function"], answer: "B", rationale: "Lerman and Iwata's comparison showed that descriptive data — because correlational rather than experimental — often failed to correctly identify the functional relationship, sometimes suggesting an incorrect function." },
      { q: "The primary distinction between ABC recording and functional analysis is that:", options: ["A. ABC recording uses shorter observation intervals than analog sessions", "B. Functional analysis requires more trained staff to run its conditions", "C. ABC recording shows correlation; functional analysis shows causation", "D. ABC recording is valid only for escape-maintained behavior"], answer: "C", rationale: "ABC recording documents what naturally precedes and follows behavior — correlational data. Functional analysis manipulates antecedent and consequence conditions experimentally to establish causal relationships." }
    ]
