      body: "Descriptive assessment is routinely summarized with a claim like \"attention followed the behavior eighty percent of the time.\" That number alone establishes nothing. A contingency is a differential relation, and a frequency count of what followed behavior cannot express one.\n\nTwo quantities are needed. The unconditional, or background, probability is how likely the consequence is across the observation generally, irrespective of the target behavior. The conditional probability is how likely it is in the moments immediately following the behavior. A contingency is indicated only when the conditional probability exceeds the unconditional one; Vollmer and colleagues termed that comparison a contingency value.\n\nThe arithmetic changes conclusions in both directions. Attention following eighty percent of episodes looks damning until you find the caregiver delivers attention eighty percent of the time regardless — the consequence is frequent but not differential, so no contingency is demonstrated. Conversely, a consequence following only fifteen percent of episodes can indicate a strong contingency when its background probability is near zero, which is why low-frequency consequences are so easily dismissed by eye.\n\nContext matters as much as arithmetic. Contingency values should be computed within the establishing operations that make the reinforcer relevant — diverted attention, instructional demands, restricted access — because a contingency operating only while an establishing operation is in effect gets diluted toward zero when averaged across a whole session. Two cautions close the method. Naturally occurring contingencies are genuinely difficult to detect, and this analysis identifies possible contingencies only. It sharpens a descriptive account; it does not convert one into an experimental demonstration."
