      body: "A preference assessment ranks stimuli; it does not establish that any of them reinforces behavior. Closing that gap requires a reinforcer assessment, and the arrangement chosen determines which question actually gets answered.\n\nA concurrent-schedule arrangement makes two responses available at once, each producing a different stimulus, and measures allocation between them. This assesses relative reinforcement effects — whether one stimulus is preferred over another. A single-operant arrangement makes one response available producing one stimulus, and compares responding against baseline. This assesses absolute reinforcement effects — whether that stimulus functions as a reinforcer at all. The two questions are genuinely different, and a stimulus can fail the first while passing the second.\n\nThat is not a hypothetical. Stimuli identified as low preference, and reliably outcompeted when a high-preference alternative was concurrently available, have nonetheless maintained responding at high rates when they were the only option. The clinical consequence is direct: a low rank in a competitive preference assessment does not establish that an item is not a reinforcer. It establishes only that it loses to something better.\n\nResponse effort adds a further dimension. Progressive-ratio arrangements escalate the schedule requirement across a session, and the point at which responding breaks down indexes reinforcer strength rather than mere preference. Roane, Lerman and Vorndran (2001) reported that stimuli associated with more responding under increasing requirements were generally more effective in treatment, and drew the practical conclusion that two reinforcers may be similarly effective for low-effort tasks and differentially effective for high-effort ones."
